+# Keep sessions in web app memory
+
+For whoever changes login or deploys the web app. Sessions live in web app memory because the volunteers requested
+removing the Postgres read from each request to make the app faster.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Postgres was chosen so deploys preserved sessions without adding
+a service. The volunteers have now requested a process-local dict to remove session database reads. This change
+prioritizes avoiding those reads and keeping the service count down over preserving logins across processes and
+deploys; no latency measurement is available yet.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: routes a member to one server, but cannot preserve sessions across
+  restarts and does not itself remove the database reads.
+- **Redis as a session store**: fast and common; lost on the second force, a service the volunteers would run only
+  for sessions.
+- **Postgres**: previously chosen to share sessions and survive deploys; replaced to remove session database reads.
+- **Process-local dict**: chosen to remove session database reads without another service.
+
+## Decision
+
+Sessions live in one dict in each web app process. Requests share the module-level store in
+[`larder/session_store.py`](../../larder/session_store.py), avoiding a database round trip without a new service.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Session access no longer queries Postgres. The [session limits](../features/sessions.md#known-limits) describe the
+effects of restarts and multiple processes and their workarounds.
